    {
      "gate": 5,
      "observed": {
        "distinct_qualified_location_ids": 0,
        "header_row": 8,
        "headers": [
          "Issues with our care directory files We are currently moving to a new digital system to manage and publish our care directory. This has caused delays in generating files in their usual format, and some updates - such as provider registration cancellations - are taking longer than normal to appear. We are working to resolve these issues and will publish an update on our website once the system changes are complete. As part of our new assessment approach, adult social care, independent health and primary care locations that provide more than one type of service no longer receive a single location-level rating. This most commonly affects locations offering services such as homecare and supported living, or GP and out-of-hours services. These locations will show as ‘Not Rated’ in this file; ratings for individual services are available in the Care directory with ratings file. Following feedback, we are reviewing this policy and will engage stakeholders on any planned changes. We’ll soon be rolling out improvements to our data platforms to resolve some known rating issues. These updates will ensure that the most recent inherited rating is displayed accurately for each service. Once the fixes are live, full details of the changes will be available in the README tab.",
          "",
          "",
          "",
          "",
          "",
          "",
          "",
          "",
          "",
          "",
          "The Care directory with filters file is a record of the legal register that CQC maintains in accordance with responsibilities laid out in the Health and Social Care Act 2008. As such, it includes instances of 'dual registration', where two providers have evidenced to CQC that they are both responsible for managing the regulated activities at a single location. In this data, these instances appear as two separate lines, with different Location IDs, but with the same names and addresses of services. As per our legal obligation, we include the number of beds associated with each of these services if they are care homes. We understand that some users of this dataset use it to understand care provision in England. To do this accurately, one half of these 'dual registered' location pairs should be removed from calculations. To support users of this data, we have created a 'Primary ID' which should be maintained for calculations of provision, the logic of which is below: The earliest registered location in each dual registered pair is identified as the Primary ID. If both locations within the pair are registered on the same date, the Location ID with the lowest number following the \"1-\" prefix is identified as the Primary ID. Users should retain the Primary ID for both calculations of provision and the number of ratings/inspections. Please note: There will be some situations where continuing regulatory history rules will mean that the earliest registered location in the pair may not have a rating. In those instances, the other rating can and should be used as a valid descriptor of the service/s provided. The full guidance on continuing regulatory history link below."
        ],
